      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Treadmills</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these treadmills with Zwift, and nothing on this page should be
            read as if we had. Our <a href="/bowflex-t6-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Bowflex T6 review</a>
            is a specification review too, not a hands-on test. The picks were made from published
            specifications, manufacturer listings and help documentation, warranty terms,
            Zwift's own support articles and independent review coverage, checked on 13 October
            2026.
          </p>
          <p>
            One rule decided eligibility: <strong>we only call a machine Zwift-compatible where
            the manufacturer or Zwift says so.</strong> Zwift publishes a list of manufacturers
            that have confirmed compatible models — Assault Fitness, Bodycraft, Bowflex, Horizon
            Fitness, JTX, Kettler, Life Fitness, NoblePro, Sole, Spirit, Star Trac, True,
            Technogym, Wahoo, Woodway and Xterra at the time of writing — and says compatibility can
            vary by model and production year. So a brand being on that list was not enough. For
            each treadmill here, the manufacturer's own product page or help centre names Zwift
            for that model.
          </p>
          <p>
            Beyond that, we looked for the things that matter for running in Zwift: a 60-inch
            deck, a top speed of at least 12 mph, an incline range that covers Zwift's hills, a
            frame and warranty built for regular running, and a price that makes sense next to the
            foot-pod alternative. A few candidates fell out on the way. One popular Sole model has
            different motor ratings and decline figures on different Sole pages, and a model whose
            Zwift support is stated only by retailers rather than by Sole itself was left off. One
            Horizon model with Zwift on its product page was showing as sold out. And one
            curved manual treadmill whose maker lists Zwift support appeared to be on pre-order, and
            we could not reach its current product page to confirm it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ftms-vs-foot-pod" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. FTMS or a Foot Pod: Which Route to Pay For</h2>
<p>
            There are two practical ways to get your speed into Zwift, and choosing between them
            is the real buying decision. The <a href="/zwift-treadmill-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Zwift guide</a>
            explains how each one works in detail; here is what each is worth when you are
            spending money.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A treadmill that broadcasts over Bluetooth FTMS</h3>
          <p>
            FTMS — the Fitness Machine Service — is an open Bluetooth standard that lets a
            treadmill send its speed, and usually incline and distance, to an app. Zwift pairs with
            a compatible FTMS treadmill directly as its run speed source: nothing on your shoe, no
            battery to forget, and no separate device to calibrate. Horizon's help documentation
            says its treadmills send Zwift speed, incline and distance. That convenience is the
            whole case for buying a connected treadmill for Zwift.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two things temper it. First, Zwift's own support article says plainly that not every
            Bluetooth-enabled treadmill is compatible — a treadmill can have Bluetooth for its own
            app and still not speak the open standard Zwift needs. "Bluetooth" on a spec sheet is
            not the same as "FTMS". Second, a treadmill broadcasts what its controller believes the
            belt speed to be. If the console reads high, Zwift records the same error with total
            confidence. You gain convenience, not accuracy.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A foot pod on any treadmill</h3>
          <p>
            A foot pod clips to your shoe and measures your stride. Zwift lists the Zwift RunPod,
            Stryd footpods, the Polar Stride Sensor and Garmin's foot pod (ANT+ only) as
            compatible. Because a pod measures you rather than the machine, it works on any
            treadmill — including brands that do not appear on Zwift's manufacturer list, such as
            NordicTrack, ProForm and Peloton, whose machines are built around their own platforms.
            Pods need calibrating, and they need to be on the shoe you are actually wearing, but
            they also travel: the same pod works on a hotel treadmill or outdoors.
          </p>
          <p>
            The decision is mostly about what you already own. <strong>If you have a treadmill you
            like, buy a pod</strong> — it is a few hundred dollars against a few thousand, and our
            <a href="/smart-treadmill-vs-regular/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">smart versus regular treadmill</a>
            comparison makes the wider case. <strong>If you are buying a treadmill anyway</strong>
            and Zwift is part of the plan, an FTMS machine costs little or nothing extra at the
            prices on this page and removes the most common set-up problem. Zwift also lists a
            treadmill sensor, the Runn, that reads belt speed on compatible machines, but the pod is
            the more flexible way to add Zwift to an existing treadmill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="auto-incline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Auto-Incline: What Zwift Actually Controls</h2>
<p>
            This is the most misunderstood part of Zwift running, and it is where marketing copy
            gets ahead of the facts.
          </p>
          <p>
            According to Zwift's own support articles, automatic incline control is available on
            one treadmill: the <strong>Wahoo KICKR RUN</strong>. With a paid Zwift subscription or
            a free trial, Zwift can raise and lower the KICKR RUN's deck to follow the gradient of
            the route. Zwift's documentation lists the conditions: incline control supports
            gradients between -3% and +15%, changes happen at about 1% per second, it is not
            available in workouts, and adjusting the incline by hand turns it off. Zwift also asks
            permission to control the treadmill at the start of each session.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every other treadmill on this page works the other way round. They <em>send</em> data to
            Zwift — speed, and in Horizon's case incline as well — but Zwift does not change their
            speed or incline. On a Horizon or a Bowflex, when the route climbs, you press the
            incline button yourself or you don't. Some manufacturers describe their FTMS connection
            as two-way and say compatible apps can send control commands; that may well be true of
            other apps, but Zwift's own documentation names only the KICKR RUN for incline control.
            Treat any listing that implies Zwift will tilt a different treadmill with caution until
            Zwift's support pages say so.
          </p>
          <p>
            Is automatic incline worth paying for? For most runners, no. Manually matching a
            route's hills is easy on a treadmill with incline dial controls, and Zwift's speed is
            what moves you around the course. Automatic incline is a genuine pleasure for someone
            who runs in Zwift most days and has the budget for a machine around $7,000. For
            everyone else, a manual 0–15% range covers every hill Zwift will show you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deck-and-speed" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Belt Length and Top Speed for Running in Zwift</h2>
<p>
            Zwift running encourages harder efforts than most treadmill sessions — group runs,
            races, a pacer to chase — so the treadmill underneath needs to be a running treadmill
            first and a connected one second.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Belt length.</strong> Sixty inches is the running standard, and every treadmill
            on this page has it. Shorter decks of 55 inches suit walkers and shorter joggers, but
            a stride lengthens as you speed up, and a race in Zwift is exactly when you would find
            the front of a short deck. Width matters too: 20 inches is fine for most runners, and
            22 inches gives more lateral margin for larger runners or anyone whose attention is on
            a screen rather than their feet. Our guide to the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills for running</a>
            covers decks in more depth, and taller runners should see the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-tall-runners/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">tall runners guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Top speed.</strong> Most home treadmills stop at 12 mph, which is a 5:00 mile.
            That covers virtually every training pace and all but the fastest intervals. The KICKR
            RUN reaches a 4:00 mile, which matters only for very fast runners. A machine that stops
            at 10 mph caps you at a 6:00 mile, which is enough for many people and a real limit for
            some. Our <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pace and speed chart</a>
            converts between the two.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline range.</strong> Zwift's controllable gradients run from -3% to +15%, and
            that is a sensible target for a manual machine too. All four treadmills here reach 15%.
            Only the KICKR RUN offers decline, which some runners value for downhill sections and
            which most home treadmills do not have.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor.</strong> We have left motor horsepower off every card on this page,
            because the figures either were not published by the manufacturer or differed between
            the manufacturer and reviewers. For running, the lifetime motor warranties on the
            Horizon and Bowflex machines are more informative than any horsepower number.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="accuracy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Accuracy: Check the Speed Before You Trust the Screen</h2>
<p>
            Whatever route you choose, Zwift believes the number it is given. That has a buying
            consequence: the cheapest Zwift set-up is not worth much if the speed feeding it is
            wrong.
          </p>
          <p>
            For an FTMS treadmill, the number Zwift receives is the console's own estimate of belt
            speed, and consoles drift, particularly under load. A new machine is usually close; an
            older one may not be. Our guide to
            <a href="/treadmill-calibration/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill calibration</a>
            explains how to check belt speed with a mark and a stopwatch, and what to do if it is
            out. It takes ten minutes and is worth doing in the first week, while the returns window
            is still open.
          </p>
          <p>
            For a foot pod, the pod itself needs calibrating, and the obvious mistake is
            calibrating it against a treadmill console you have not checked. Check the belt first,
            then calibrate the pod to the belt. Zwift's own documentation recommends calibrating a
            footpod before you use it, and our guide on
            <a href="/is-my-treadmill-speed-accurate/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether your treadmill speed is accurate</a>
            covers why the two rarely agree out of the box.
          </p>
          <p>
            Heart rate is a separate signal, usually from a chest strap or arm band paired to
            Zwift directly, and handrail grips are no use for it while you run. If you want it on
            screen, our pick of the
            <a href="/best-heart-rate-monitors-for-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best heart rate monitors for treadmill training</a>
            covers which straps can talk to Zwift and a watch at the same time.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            A Zwift-friendly treadmill is a good choice for runners who will use Zwift regularly
            and are buying a treadmill anyway. It is the wrong purchase in several common
            situations.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you already own a working treadmill,</strong> do not replace it for Zwift.
            Buy a foot pod. The treadmill does not need to know Zwift exists.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want classes and a screen rather than Zwift,</strong> you are shopping
            for a different machine. Built-in platforms such as iFIT and Peloton are covered in our
            <a href="/best-smart-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best smart treadmills</a>
            roundup. Those machines generally do not appear on Zwift's manufacturer list, so a pod
            is how you would add Zwift to one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you mostly walk,</strong> a full running treadmill is more machine than you
            need, and the deck length and top speed that make these machines good for Zwift running
            are features you would be paying for and not using. The <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads with incline</a>
            page and the <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmills guide</a>
            are better starting points.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>If you want no ongoing costs at all,</strong> remember that Zwift itself is a
            separate service with its own terms, and automatic incline on the KICKR RUN requires a
            paid Zwift subscription. Every treadmill here works fully without any subscription, and
            our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>
            covers that priority directly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which treadmills work with Zwift?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Zwift lists manufacturers that have confirmed compatible models, including Horizon, Bowflex, Sole, Spirit, Life Fitness, Technogym, True, Woodway, Xterra and Wahoo, and says compatibility can vary by model and production year. Check that the manufacturer names Zwift for the exact model. Any treadmill at all works with Zwift if you add a compatible foot pod.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can Zwift control my treadmill’s incline?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only on one treadmill, according to Zwift's support documentation: the Wahoo KICKR RUN, with a paid Zwift subscription or free trial. Zwift adjusts it between -3% and +15% and hands control back if you touch the incline yourself. Other compatible treadmills send their speed, and sometimes incline, to Zwift, but you change their incline by hand.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a foot pod as good as an FTMS treadmill for Zwift?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For getting your speed into Zwift, yes, once it is calibrated. A pod measures your stride, so it works on any treadmill and travels with you; an FTMS treadmill broadcasts its own belt speed with nothing on your shoe. Neither is automatically more accurate. The treadmill route is more convenient; the pod route is far cheaper if you already own a treadmill.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Bluetooth on a treadmill mean it works with Zwift?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Zwift's own support article says not all Bluetooth-enabled treadmills are compatible. Many treadmills use Bluetooth only for their maker's own app. What Zwift needs is the open fitness-machine standard, usually listed as Bluetooth FTMS, or a manufacturer statement naming Zwift. If a listing just says Bluetooth, ask before buying.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use Zwift on a NordicTrack treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                NordicTrack and ProForm do not appear on Zwift's list of manufacturers with confirmed compatible treadmills, because their machines are built around iFIT. The reliable route is a foot pod from Zwift's compatible list, such as a Stryd, which works on any treadmill regardless of brand. Check belt speed first so you calibrate the pod against an accurate figure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What deck size do I need for Zwift running?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 60-inch running deck, which every treadmill on this page has. Zwift encourages harder efforts — group runs, races, chasing a pacer — and a stride lengthens as you speed up, so a 55-inch deck that suits jogging becomes cramped. A 22-inch width gives larger runners more margin while their attention is on the screen.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Treadmill for Zwift</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Two of these cost money for no benefit, and two produce Zwift data that looks right and is not.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Replacing a good treadmill just for Zwift</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Zwift needs a speed signal, not a particular treadmill. If the machine you own runs well, a foot pod from Zwift’s compatible list gives you the same experience for a fraction of the price of a new treadmill. Buy an FTMS treadmill only when you are replacing the treadmill anyway.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Zwift will change the incline</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Zwift’s documentation names one treadmill whose incline it controls: the Wahoo KICKR RUN. Every other compatible treadmill sends data to Zwift but does not take instructions from it. If automatic hills are the reason you are buying, check Zwift’s support pages for the exact model, not the treadmill’s marketing copy.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading “Bluetooth” as “Zwift compatible”</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Many treadmills have Bluetooth only for their own app, and Zwift says plainly that not every Bluetooth treadmill is compatible. Look for Bluetooth FTMS, or a statement from the manufacturer naming Zwift for that model and production year, before you pay for connectivity you cannot use.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting the console speed without checking it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An FTMS treadmill sends Zwift whatever its console believes, and a foot pod calibrated against that console inherits the same error. Check the real belt speed with a marked belt and a stopwatch in the first week, while you can still return the machine, then calibrate your pod to that figure.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

